* =============================================================
* pot_log.lib
* Logarithmic (audio-taper) potentiometer model for ngspice / KiCad
* =============================================================
*
* Unlike a simple fixed-resistor stand-in, this model takes the wiper
* rotation position as a parameter, so sweeping POS from 0 to 1
* reproduces how the resistance divider actually changes as you turn
* the knob -- including the non-linear "audio taper" curve.
*
* Pins:
*   A - end terminal 1
*   W - wiper (center) terminal
*   B - end terminal 2
*
* Parameters:
*   RTOTAL - total end-to-end resistance of the pot (e.g. 500k)
*   POS    - wiper position, 0.0 (fully at A) .. 1.0 (fully at B)
*   K      - taper exponent.
*              K=1    -> linear taper (same as a plain linear pot)
*              K~3.32 -> standard "audio"/log taper
*                        (50% rotation gives ~10% of total resistance,
*                        since 0.5^3.32 ~= 0.1)
*            Tune K to match the datasheet's rotation-vs-resistance
*            curve if you have one; higher K = more of the change is
*            crammed into the last part of the rotation.
*   RMIN   - small floor resistance at each end, avoids a 0-ohm branch
*            at POS=0 or POS=1 which can cause convergence issues
*
* Usage in a .cir file:
*   .include pot_log.lib
*   XPOT_TH A_node W_node B_node POT_LOG PARAMS: RTOTAL=500k POS=0.5 K=3.32
*
* Usage from KiCad's Simulation Model Editor (per-symbol):
*   Model:   SUBCKT
*   Library: pot_log.lib
*   Name:    POT_LOG
*   Pin Assignments (map to your symbol's actual pin numbering):
*       Symbol Pin 1 -> A
*       Symbol Pin 2 -> B
*       Symbol Pin 3 -> W      (do NOT leave this as "Not Connected")
*   Sim.Params: RTOTAL=500k POS=0.5 K=3.32
*
* To sweep rotation in spice_eval.py or a manual .cir, just override
* POS at the top level (e.g. via .param POS=... and .step param POS
* list 0 0.1 0.2 ... 1.0) instead of hand-editing two resistor values.
*
* Which end is "A" vs "B" only affects which direction of rotation
* (CW vs CCW) makes the resistance rise faster -- it does not change
* correctness. If the simulated threshold behavior comes out backwards
* from what you want on the physical knob, swap the A/B connections;
* no need to touch this file.
* =============================================================

.subckt POT_LOG A W B PARAMS: RTOTAL=500k POS=0.5 K=3.32 RMIN=10
R_AW A W R={RTOTAL*(POS**K) + RMIN}
R_WB W B R={RTOTAL*(1-(POS**K)) + RMIN}
.ends POT_LOG

* -------------------------------------------------------------
* Linear-taper version, included for direct A/B comparison against
* POT_LOG at the same POS value (K=1 case, spelled out explicitly
* rather than relying on remembering to pass K=1).
* -------------------------------------------------------------
.subckt POT_LIN A W B PARAMS: RTOTAL=500k POS=0.5 RMIN=10
R_AW A W R={RTOTAL*POS + RMIN}
R_WB W B R={RTOTAL*(1-POS) + RMIN}
.ends POT_LIN
